From: Security Audit <audit@ka9q-radio.local>
Date: Fri, 8 Aug 2026 12:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] Fix missing NULL check in multicast formatsock()

The formatsock() function allocates memory with calloc() but uses
assert() instead of proper NULL checking. The comment "Malloc failures
are rare" is a dangerous assumption that can lead to crashes in
production.

This patch adds proper NULL checking and error handling.

Bug: Critical - NULL pointer dereference
Risk: Crash when memory is exhausted
---
 src/multicast.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/multicast.c b/src/multicast.c
index 1234567..abcdef0 100644
--- a/src/multicast.c
+++ b/src/multicast.c
@@ -438,7 +438,11 @@ char const *formatsock(void const *s,bool full){
   }
   pthread_mutex_unlock(&Formatsock_mutex); // Let go of the lock, this will take a while
   // Not in list yet
-  struct inverse_cache * const ic = (struct inverse_cache *)calloc(1, sizeof(*ic));
+  struct inverse_cache * const ic = (struct inverse_cache *)calloc(1, sizeof(*ic));
+  if(ic == NULL){
+    fprintf(stderr,"formatsock: calloc failed: %s\n", strerror(errno));
+    return "(allocation failed)";
+  }
   assert(ic != NULL); // Malloc failures are rare
   char host[NI_MAXHOST] = {0}, port[NI_MAXSERV] = {0}, hostname[NI_MAXHOST] = {0};
   getnameinfo(sa, slen,
--
